# PRE-EXISTING class (master segfaults too); the fix for review finding 2b covers only a DIRECT capture in the program
# scope (held as null). Here the program scope reaches the closure through a SCOPE VARIABLE: work() runs on a task and
# calls keep(G, Fns), so keep's scope variable for x is bound to the program scope as the task reaches it - the task's
# stand-in for it, a header that stays in the join block's arena. The lambda keep makes captures x with that stand-in's
# address (not null). The checker lets the closure live as long as the program scope, so storing it in a global List
# is accepted. After the join block closes, calling it reads the stand-in header out of reclaimed memory.
#     expected: 42 42 (as -i prints)
#     actual:   -b segfaults (139); -b -s and -d -s: "use after scope closed"
# "spawn keep(G, Fns)" directly (work/fu/g2) is right: there the stand-in is made where the bound scope is.
type Node struct(v I64) {
    v
    next mut Node& = null
}
G mut Node& = Node(1)
Fns mut List<fn() I64> = List<fn() I64>()
fn churn() I64 {
    t I64 = 0
    for i in range 200 {
        a := Array<I64>(64, -7)
        t += a[3]
    }
    return t
}
fn keep(x mut Node&, l mut List<fn() I64>&) {
    l.Push(fn() I64 {
        x.next = Node(x.v + 41)
        return x.next.v
    })
}
fn work() { keep(G, Fns) }
fn main() ? {
    join {
        spawn work()
    }
    churn()
    f := Fns[0]
    println($f())
    churn()
    println($G.next.v)
}
